  [MEDIUM] PE-SECTION-VIRTUAL-ONLY  Executable section with no data on disk  (confidence: medium, packing)
      An executable section occupies memory but has no bytes in the file: it is filled at run time, the classic layout of packers (e.g. UPX0) that decompress code into it. Uninitialized-data sections (.bss) are not executable and are not reported.
      - section #0 "UPX0"
      - VirtualSize = 0x20000 (SizeOfRawData 0)
      (explained by UPX via PE-PACKER-KNOWN: not scored)
  [LOW] PE-TLS-CALLBACKS  TLS callbacks run before the entry point  (confidence: medium, anti_analysis)
      1 TLS callback(s) execute before the entry point, so a debugger breaking at the entry point misses them; used for anti-debugging and early unpacking. Rust and MinGW runtimes also register TLS callbacks.
      - callback = 0x401000 in #0 UPX0
  [LOW] PE-ENTRYPOINT-UNUSUAL  Unusual entry point location  (confidence: low, anomaly)
      The entry point is in an executable section other than the first one. Compilers put the entry point in the first code section; packers and protectors move it to their own stub.
      - AddressOfEntryPoint = 0x00021100
      - section #1 "UPX1"
      - first executable section = #0 UPX0
